.SH NAME
mail, rmail \- send and receive mail among users
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmail\fR [\fB\-pqr\fR] [\fB\-f\fI file\fR]
.PP
\fBmail\fI persons\fR
.PP
\fBrmail\fI persons\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Mail\^
without arguments prints a user's mail, message-by-message,
in last-in, first-out order.
For each message, the user is prompted with a
.BR ? ,
and a line is read from the standard input
to determine the disposition of the message:
.TP
.B d
Delete message and go on to next message.
.TP
.B "EOT (control-D)"
Put undeleted mail back in the
.I mailfile\^ 
and stop.
.TP
.BR m [\fIpersons\fR]
Mail the message to the named
.I persons\^
(yourself is default).
.TP 16
<new-line>
Go on to next message.
.TP
.B p
Print message again.
.TP
.B q
Same as
.BR EOT ,
and stop.
.TP
.BR s [\fIfile\fR]
Save message in the named
.I files\^
.RB ( mbox
is default).
.TP
.BR w [\fIfiles\fR]
Save message, without its header, in the named
.I files\^
.RB ( mbox
is default).
.TP
.B x
Put all mail back in the
.I mailfile\^
unchanged and stop.
.TP
.B +
Same as <new-line>.
.TP
.B \-
Go back to previous message.
.TP
.BI ! command\^
Escape to the shell to do
.IR command .
.TP
.B \(**
Print a command summary.
.PP
When \fIpersons\fR are named,
.B mail\^
takes the standard input up to an end-of-file
(or a line consisting of just a
.BR \&. )
and adds it to each
.IR person 's
.IR mailfile .
The message is preceded by the sender's name and a postmark.
Lines that look like postmarks
in the message,
(i.e., "From\.\.\.")
are preceded with a
.BR > .
A
.I person\^
is usually a user name recognized by
.BR login (1).
If a
.I person\^
being sent mail is not recognized, or if
.B mail\^
is interrupted during input, the
.B dead.letter
will be saved
to allow editing and resending.
It will be placed in the current working directory.
.PP
To denote a recipient on a remote system, prefix
.I person\^
by the system name and exclamation mark (see
.BR uucp (1)).
Everything after the first exclamation mark in
.I persons\^
is interpreted by the remote system.
In particular, if
.I persons\^
contains additional exclamation marks, it can denote a sequence
of machines through which the message is to be sent on the way
to its ultimate destination.
For example, specifying
.B a!b!cde
as a recipient's name causes the message to be sent to user
.B b!cde
on system
.BR a .
System
.B a
will interpret that destination as a request to send the
message to user
.B cde
on system
.BR b .
This is useful if the sending system can access system
.B a
but not system
.BR b ,
and system
.B a
has access to system
.BR b .
.PP
The
.I mailfile\^
can be manipulated in two ways to alter the function of
.BR mail .
The
.I other\^
permissions of the file may be read-write, read-only, or neither
read nor write to allow different levels of privacy.
If changed to other than the default, the file will be preserved
even when empty to perpetuate the desired permissions.
The file can also contain the first line:
.PP
.RS
Forward to
.I person\^
.RE
.PP
which causes all mail sent to the owner of the
.I mailfile\^
to be forwarded to
.IR person .
This is especially useful to forward all of a person's mail
to one machine in a multiple machine environment.
.PP
.B Rmail\^
only permits the sending of mail;
.BR uucp (1)
uses
.B rmail\^
as a security precaution.
.PP
When a user logs in he is informed of the presence
of mail, if any.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB \-f \fIfile\^\fR
causes
.B mail\^
to use
.I file\^
(e.g.,
.BR mbox )
instead of the default
.IR mailfile .
.bp
.TP
.B \-p
causes all mail to be printed without prompting for disposition.
.TP
.B \-q
causes
.B mail\^
to terminate after interrupts.
Normally an interrupt only causes the
termination of the message being printed.
.TP
.B \-r
causes messages to be printed in first-in, first-out order.
.SH FILES
.ta  28
/etc/passwd	to identify persons
.br
/usr/spool/mail/\(**	incoming mail for user \(**
.br
$HOME/mbox	saved mail
.br
$MAIL	\fImailfile\fR
.br
/tmp/ma\(**	temporary file
.br
/usr/spool/mail/\(**.lock	lock for mail directory
.br
dead.letter	unmailable text
.br
.ta
.SH SEE ALSO
login(1),
uucp(1),
write(1).
.SH LIMITATIONS
Race conditions sometimes result in a failure to remove a lock file.
.br
After an interrupt, the next message may not be printed;
printing may be forced by typing a
.BR p .
